.AVIATION...
(12Z TAFS)
Issued at 520 AM PDT Fri Aug 7 2026

Mid and high clouds are moving northward over the forecast area
and there may be a few high based light showers/virga based on
KMUX data. Strong 500 mb high pressure continues and the lower
level temperature inversion prevails in the hills and mountains.
At the same time, the marine layer is well established with a cool
air intrusion on northwest winds along the coastline and into the
nearby coastal valleys. The SFO-SAC pressure gradient is 2.9 mb,
1 mb greater than 24 hours ago, a decent onshore gradient for 5
am.

At the moment the overall pattern is stable and not likely to
become disrupted. Even though mid and high clouds are present and
slowing radiative cooling, cool air advection is taking place
within the marine layer, supporting stratus, fog and mist /MVFR-
IFR-LIFR/. It`s favorable thermodynamic conditions e.g. at the
air/sea interface for stratus and fog and btw, kudos to the NAM
1000 mb forecasts for helping to quickly identify (and observations
to verify) this shallow cool air intrusion. Cloud ceilings have
lowered to IFR-LIFR during the overnight hours because of favorable
air/sea conditions and a slight decrease in the marine layer depth.

This complex cloud pattern mix will carry through morning, the
question is how long will it take to mix out the stratus, fog and
mist under August sun angle surface heating? Some delay in surface
heating means potential delay for stratus, fog and mist to mix out
to VFR. Interestingly, weak thermal troughing is located at the
500 mb level today and is slow to move northward out of the
forecast area. Timing mix out is a low confidence forecast, with
uncertainty will go with late morning mix out, but it may also take
another couple hours to fully mix out along the coastline. The NAM
forecasts upwards of 4 mb SFO-SAC pressure gradient in the afternoon.
So far the marine layer has essentially been deeper compared to model
forecasts. Satellite shows widespread stratus and fog over the coastal
and offshore waters. With onshore winds dominating, expect stratus,
fog and mist /MVFR-IFR-LIFR/ to return inland tonight and Saturday
morning.

Vicinity of SFO...Stratus /IFR/ until 18z today, but caveat is
the current combination of onshore winds, a cooler air intrusion,
mid and high clouds may end up slowing mix out to VFR by another
hour or two today. Stratus /IFR/ is forecast to return mid evening
and continue tonight and Saturday morning. West to northwest wind
5 to 15 knots.

SFO Bridge Approach...Similar to SFO.

Monterey Bay Terminals...Stratus /IFR-LIFR/ improving to MVFR by
late morning. The surface cool air intrusion affecting vicinity
KSFO, KOAK, KHAF and areas southward along the coast is still
arriving here at this hour. While mid and high clouds are drifting
overhead, this will at least temporarily slow diurnal warming while
the cooler air undercuts the lower level temperature inversion.
Caveat, here too it may take another couple hours for stratus
/IFR-LIFR/ to lift to MVFR-VFR which may turn out to be by early
afternoon. West to northwest winds 5 to 15 knots.

